---

## Objectives

- [ ] Understand the BST property and its implications for search {due=2026-10-24}
- [ ] State and prove the search time complexity
- [ ] Compare balanced and unbalanced trees

---

## Definitions

:::definition {title="Binary tree" #def:binary-tree}
A *binary tree* is a rooted tree where every node has at most two children,
called the **left child** and the **right child**.
:::

:::definition {title="Binary search tree" #def:bst}
A *binary search tree* (BST) is a [binary tree]{arrow=def:binary-tree} $T$ such that for every node $v$:

$$\text{key}(u) < \text{key}(v) \quad \forall u \in \text{left-subtree}(v)$$
$$\text{key}(w) > \text{key}(v) \quad \forall w \in \text{right-subtree}(v)$$

This is the *BST property*.
:::

The BST property means an in-order traversal visits keys in sorted order —
see [@thm:inorder] below.

Annotating like you would on paper: [the BST property]{arrow=def:bst} draws
a connector back to its definition above in the preview, and this note wires up several more below, like a diagram sketched over the text.

---

## Theorems and proofs

:::theorem {title="Search complexity" #thm:bst-search}
Search, insert and delete in a BST each run in $O(h)$ time, where $h$ is the
[height]{arrow=def:height} of the tree.
:::

:::proof
Each operation descends from the root, taking one step per level and making
a binary decision at each node. It therefore visits at most $h + 1$ nodes.
:::

:::theorem {title="In-order traversal" #thm:inorder}
An in-order traversal of a BST visits all keys in non-decreasing order.
:::

:::proof
By induction on the number of nodes. The base case (empty tree) is trivial.
For a non-empty tree, the BST property guarantees all keys in the left
subtree are smaller than the root, which are smaller than all in the right
subtree. Applying the induction hypothesis to each subtree gives the result.
:::

:::remark
A randomly built BST has expected height $O(\log n)$.
An adversarially ordered input gives a degenerate tree with $h = n - 1$,
so worst-case complexity is $O(n)$.
For guaranteed $O(\log n)$, use a [balanced]{arrow=def:balanced arrow-style=curve} variant: AVL tree, red-black tree,
or a skip list. See [@cormen2022] for details.
:::

---

## Examples

:::example {title="Searching for 5" #ex:bst-search}
Given the BST built from the sequence `[8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14, 4, 7]`,
search for key 5:

1. 5 < 8 → go left to 3
2. 5 > 3 → go right to 6
3. 5 < 6 → go left to 4
4. 5 > 4 → go right — `nil`, not found

The search makes 4 comparisons. The tree's [height]{arrow=def:height} is 3, so this is $O(h)$.
:::

:::example {title="Insertion" #ex:bst-insert}
Insert key 5 into the same tree. Follow the [same path as search]{arrow=ex:bst-search} — when you
reach `nil`, attach the new node there (as the right child of 4).
:::

---

## Derivation

:::proof
**Show that insertion preserves the BST property.**

Let $T$ be a BST and $k$ a new key. Insert $k$ by the standard algorithm,
which places it at a leaf position. Call this leaf $\ell$.

By construction, at every ancestor $v$ of $\ell$:
- We went left at $v$, so $k < \text{key}(v)$, and $\ell$ is in
  $v$'s left subtree. ✓
- Or we went right at $v$, so $k > \text{key}(v)$, and $\ell$ is in
  $v$'s right subtree. ✓

The BST property at $\ell$ is vacuously satisfied (it has no children).
Therefore $T$ remains a BST after insertion.
:::

