## Practice questions

:::question {#q:height}
What is the [height]{arrow=def:height} of a BST built by inserting the keys `[5, 3, 7, 1, 4, 6, 8]`
in that order? Draw the tree.
:::

:::question {#q:degenerate}
Give a sequence of $n$ distinct keys whose insertion produces a BST with
height $n - 1$. Why is this problematic in practice?
:::

---

## Key terms

:::definition {title="Height" #def:height}
The *height* of a tree is the length of the longest path from the root to a leaf.
An empty tree has height $-1$; a single node has height $0$.
:::

:::definition {title="Balanced BST" #def:balanced}
A BST is *balanced* if the heights of the two subtrees of every node differ
by at most 1. Examples: AVL tree, red–black tree.
:::

---

## Review cards

:::card
What is the BST property?

---

For every node $v$: left subtree keys < key($v$) < right subtree keys.
:::

:::card
What does an in-order BST traversal produce?

---

The keys in sorted (non-decreasing) order.
:::

:::card
What is the worst-case height of a BST built from $n$ keys?

---

$n - 1$, when keys are inserted in sorted order.
:::

:::card {#card:search-complexity}
What is the time complexity of BST search, and when is it achieved?

---

**$O(h)$** where $h$ is the tree's height.

- Average case (random input): $O(\log n)$
- Worst case (sorted input): $O(n)$
- Guaranteed $O(\log n)$: use a balanced BST (AVL or red–black)
:::

**Cloze cards** — run *YAMD: Toggle Quiz Mode* to hide the answers:

An in-order traversal of a BST visits keys in :cloze[sorted order].

A BST built from sorted input has height :cloze[$n - 1$], giving :cloze[$O(n)$] search.

The AVL tree and the :cloze[red–black tree] are [balanced BST variants]{arrow=def:balanced}.

---

## Summary

:::summary
{#summary-core}
A binary search tree organises keys so that search, insert and delete each cost
$O(h)$ time, where $h$ is the tree's height. On random input $h = O(\log n)$;
on sorted input $h = O(n)$. Balanced variants (AVL, red–black) guarantee
$O(\log n)$ regardless of insertion order. The [in-order traversal]{arrow=thm:inorder} visits keys
in sorted order — a property that follows directly from the BST invariant.
:::

